# The two environment variables the game must be launched with on this machine.
#
# SOURCED, NOT RUN. There is no #! line and no exec: `. tools/gpu-offload` from
# the shell that is about to start the game. Every launch path in the tree does
# it — the Makefile's run, run-debug, run-release and headless targets, and
# `tools/debug-api run` — and gpu_offload_test.cpp fails the build if one of them
# stops.
#
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY, AND THE SYMPTOM THAT MATTERS
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This machine has two GPUs and the desktop runs off the NVIDIA one. Without
# PRIME render offload, DXVK enumerates and picks the OTHER card, then cannot
# build a swapchain that the NVIDIA-driven display can present, and the process
# takes SIGTRAP inside hl2.sh:
#
#   info:  Device properties:
#   info:    Device : AMD Radeon RX 9070 XT (RADV GFX1201)
#   ...
#   err:   D3D9SwapChainEx: Failed to recreate swap chain: VK_ERROR_INITIALIZATION_FAILED
#   hl2.sh: line 113: ... Trace/breakpoint trap (core dumped)
#
# THE SIGNATURE IS ZERO `[SourceSharp]` LINES. That is the detail worth
# remembering, and the reason this file exists rather than a note somebody was
# meant to have read. The failure is in the renderer, which comes up BEFORE
# server.so is loaded and long before the managed host starts — so the log holds
# not one line of ours, `tools/debug-api status` never answers because no socket is
# ever created, and the whole thing reads exactly like "the port change I just
# made broke module init". It has cost this project real work: a sound lift
# shipped with ss_sandbox's emitters unexercised, a lane abandoned live
# verification entirely, and three attempts to verify -assertstack live failed
# for what was almost certainly this.
#
# `-textmode` does not dodge it. It fails identically, which removes the obvious
# workaround and is why "just run it headless" is not the answer.
#
# A run that HAS the variables says so, in the same block:
#
#   info:  Device properties:
#   info:    Device : NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2070 SUPER
#
# If a launch dies with no `[SourceSharp]` line at all, read the Device line
# before reading anything else.
#
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OVERRIDING IT
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Nothing here is forced. Both variables are set only if they are not already
# set, so the environment always wins:
#
#   SS_GPU_OFFLOAD=0 make run              set neither — a single-GPU machine
#   __GLX_VENDOR_LIBRARY_NAME=mesa make run   drive the other card instead
#   SS_GPU_OFFLOAD=0 tools/debug-api run          the same, for debug-api
#
# SS_GPU_OFFLOAD=0 exists because "unset it" is not expressible on a command
# line: a variable this file would default anyway cannot be turned off by
# clearing it. On a machine with one GPU the variables are harmless rather than
# wrong — the NVIDIA GLX vendor library simply is not there and the loader falls
# back — but an explicit off switch beats asking anyone to find out.

if [ "${SS_GPU_OFFLOAD:-1}" != "0" ]; then
	: "${__NV_PRIME_RENDER_OFFLOAD:=1}"
	: "${__GLX_VENDOR_LIBRARY_NAME:=nvidia}"
	export __NV_PRIME_RENDER_OFFLOAD
	export __GLX_VENDOR_LIBRARY_NAME
fi
